      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Myotonia characterized by persistent contraction or delayed relaxation of muscle fibers on initiation of movement or when stimulated to contract.",
              "pdf_page": 1026,
              "printed_page": 944,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "May affect all skeletal muscles.",
              "pdf_page": 1026,
              "printed_page": 944,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Myotonia may be congenital or acquired.",
              "pdf_page": 1026,
              "printed_page": 944,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "A genetic basis has been determined in some breeds.",
              "pdf_page": 1026,
              "printed_page": 944,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Acquired myotonia may be associated with noninflammatory or inflammatory myopathy.",
              "pdf_page": 1026,
              "printed_page": 944,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Neuromyotonia and myokymia, characterized by continuous muscle fiber activity, are believed to be due to hyperexcitability of terminal nerve branches (neuromyotonia) or hyperexcitability of the motor nerve axon at any level (myokymia) rather than a primary muscle disorder.",
              "pdf_page": 1026,
              "printed_page": 944,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Neuromyotonia is characterized by muscle stiffness and delayed relaxation.",
              "pdf_page": 1026,
              "printed_page": 944,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Myokymia describes rhythmic undulating muscle movements that produce a rippling movement of the skin.",
              "pdf_page": 1026,
              "printed_page": 944,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "The cause of neuromyotonia and myokymia (continuous muscle fiber activity [CMFA]) in all reported cases remains unclear.",
              "pdf_page": 1026,
              "printed_page": 944,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "CMFA and spinocerebellar ataxia may occur concurrently in Jack Russell terriers (JRTs) (and related breeds) and both are associated with a genetic mutation causing neuronal ion channel dysfunction.",
              "pdf_page": 1026,
              "printed_page": 944,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
